          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.795,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta’s Formula Generalises Heron’s Formula"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.137,
            0.886,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The similarity in appearance between Heron’s formula and Brahmagupta’s formula is not an accident. How? Brahmagupta’s formula generalises Heron’s formula. That is, we can think of Heron’s formula as a special case of Brahmagupta’s formula."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.215,
            0.886,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A triangle with sides a, b, c can be regarded as a ‘special case’ of a 4-gon ABCD whose fourth side d has zero length (d = 0). So we have "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "AB = a,   BC = b,   CD = c,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , which means in effect that vertices A and D coincide."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.292,
            0.886,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(If the vertices represent planets, then this would be a case of two planets colliding with each other and forming a single large planet! This actually happened in the early history of the solar system.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.35,
            0.886,
            0.408
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since any triangle is cyclic (given any three points not in a straight line, we can draw a circle through them), this 4-gon is cyclic too. So, Brahmagupta’s formula must apply to this 4-gon."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.407,
            0.885,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us see what emerges from this. We first compute the semi-perimeter of the 4-gon (keep in mind that d = 0):"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.33,
            0.458,
            0.667,
            0.5
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "s = \\frac{1}{2}(a + b + c + 0) = \\frac{1}{2}(a + b + c),"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.51,
            0.883,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We see that s is the same as the semi-perimeter of the given triangle. Now we apply Brahmagupta’s formula:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.563,
            0.712,
            0.636
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_22_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{Area}   of triangle =\\sqrt{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)} \\\\ =\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}   since   d=0."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.654,
            0.417,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This is Heron’s formula!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.679,
            0.886,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Brahmagupta’s formula may thus be viewed as a generalisation of Heron’s formula."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.743,
            0.483,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.9 Squaring a Rectangle"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.777,
            0.887,
            0.894
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In ancient times, to ‘square a given shape’ meant to ‘construct a square equal in area to that shape’. The shape could be a rectangle or a triangle or a circle. Here, we show how the ancient Indian mathematician Baudhāyana squared a rectangle. The construction, from his Śhulbasūtra (800 BCE), shows how to square a rectangle with sides a units and b units where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a   \\ge   b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . This means that we must construct"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
